//? Title: Compositional log-ratio analysis
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Analyse parts of a whole through log-ratios: centered, additive and isometric coordinates, the Aitchison distance, coherent summaries, and principled replacement of zeros

Data that carry only relative information, such as budget shares, mineral or microbial abundances, time-use or vote shares, are **compositions**. Their total is either fixed or uninformative, so the parts are constrained and cannot vary independently. Correlations between raw proportions are distorted by this constraint (Pearson 1897; Chayes 1960). They also change when parts are added or removed, so conclusions about Food and Other expenditures depend on whether Housing was recorded. Aitchison (1982, 1986) resolved this by analysing log-ratios, which depend only on relative information and are unchanged by rescaling or by dropping other parts.

The log-ratio coordinates map the simplex to real space:

- **clr**: \(\log x_j-\overline{\log x}\) is symmetric in the parts, but its \(D\) coordinates sum to zero, so their covariance is singular.
- **alr**: \(\log(x_j/x_D)\) gives \(D-1\) unconstrained coordinates, but distances depend on the choice of reference part.
- **ilr**: \(\mathrm{clr}(x)\,V\), for any \(D\times(D-1)\) contrast matrix \(V\) with orthonormal columns, gives real coordinates that preserve distances and angles. A **sequential binary partition** produces interpretable *balances*, \(\sqrt{rs/(r+s)}\,\log(g(x_+)/g(x_-))\), contrasting the geometric means of two groups of parts.

The **Aitchison distance** is the Euclidean distance between clr vectors. Summaries follow from the same geometry: the center is the closed geometric mean, the **variation matrix** is \(\tau_{jk}=\mathrm{var}\log(x_j/x_k)\), and the total variance is \(\sum\tau_{jk}/(2D)\).

Log-ratios cannot handle zeros, so zeros must be replaced in a way that depends on their origin. **Rounded zeros**, values below a detection limit, are replaced by a fraction of that limit, and the other parts are rescaled multiplicatively so that their ratios are unchanged (Martín-Fernández et al. 2003). **Count zeros** come from finite sampling of a positive proportion. They are replaced by posterior estimates under a Dirichlet prior built from the other samples: the geometric Bayesian (GBM), square-root (SQ) and Bayes–Laplace (BL) priors, or by count-zero multiplicative replacement (CZM). All four come from Martín-Fernández, Hron, Templ, Filzmoser and Palarea-Albaladejo (2015).

The module `stats/compositional.hpp` provides all of these. Its tests reproduce `compositions` (clr, alr, ilr, `ilrBase`, `gsi.buildilrBase`, `variation`, `mvar`, the center and `dist`) and `zCompositions` (`multRepl`, and `cmultRepl` with each prior, with and without adjustment) to rounding. They also check every inverse, the orthonormality of each basis, and the identity linking the pivot basis to the Helmert basis.

The example uses Aitchison's household expenditures (20 men and 20 women, four commodity groups) and the `Pigs` location counts (29 sows, six locations, many zeros). The simulation compares zero-replacement strategies on multinomial counts drawn from known logistic-normal compositions.

```{cpp, id=coda, file="figures/log_ratio.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/distributions.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/compositional.hpp>

#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <fstream>
#include <functional>
#include <print>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using Matrix = dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>;

std::vector<std::vector<double>> read_rows(const std::string& path) {
    std::ifstream in(path);
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> rows;
    for (std::string line; std::getline(in, line);) {
        if (line.empty() || line[0] == '#') continue;
        std::istringstream s(line);
        rows.emplace_back();
        for (double v; s >> v;) rows.back().push_back(v);
    }
    return rows;
}

Matrix columns(const std::vector<std::vector<double>>& rows, const std::vector<int>& cols) {
    Matrix m(rows.size(), cols.size(), 0.0);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rows.size(); ++i)
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < cols.size(); ++j) m(i, j) = rows[i][cols[j]];
    return m;
}

double correlation(const std::vector<double>& a, const std::vector<double>& b) {
    const double n = static_cast<double>(a.size());
    double ma = 0, mb = 0, sab = 0, saa = 0, sbb = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) ma += a[i] / n, mb += b[i] / n;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) {
        sab += (a[i] - ma) * (b[i] - mb);
        saa += (a[i] - ma) * (a[i] - ma), sbb += (b[i] - mb) * (b[i] - mb);
    }
    return sab / std::sqrt(saa * sbb);
}

double euclid(const std::vector<double>& a, const std::vector<double>& b) {
    double s = 0;
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < a.size(); ++j) s += (a[j] - b[j]) * (a[j] - b[j]);
    return std::sqrt(s);
}

int main() {
    const auto house = read_rows("data/household.txt");
    const auto x = columns(house, {0, 1, 2, 3});  // Housing, Food, Other, Services
    const auto sub = columns(house, {1, 2, 3});   // drop Housing
    const auto p = dm::stats::closure(x), ps = dm::stats::closure(sub);

    // 1. Coherence: share correlations change with the subcomposition; log-ratios do not.
    const auto tf = dm::stats::variation_matrix(x), ts = dm::stats::variation_matrix(sub);
    std::println("| Pair | Share correlation, 4 parts | Share correlation, Housing "
                 "dropped | var log-ratio, 4 parts | var log-ratio, Housing dropped |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const char* names[]{"Housing", "Food", "Other", "Services"};
    for (const auto& [j, k] : {std::pair{1, 2}, std::pair{1, 3}, std::pair{2, 3}})
        std::println("| {}/{} | {:+.3f} | {:+.3f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |", names[j], names[k],
                     correlation(p.col(j), p.col(k)),
                     correlation(ps.col(j - 1), ps.col(k - 1)), tf(j, k), ts(j - 1, k - 1));

    // Subcompositional dominance: distances should not grow when parts are dropped.
    int euclid_grows = 0, aitchison_grows = 0, pairs = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.rows(); ++i)
        for (std::size_t k = i + 1; k < x.rows(); ++k, ++pairs) {
            euclid_grows += euclid(ps.row(i), ps.row(k)) > euclid(p.row(i), p.row(k));
            aitchison_grows += dm::stats::aitchison_distance(sub.row(i), sub.row(k)) >
                               dm::stats::aitchison_distance(x.row(i), x.row(k)) + 1e-12;
        }
    std::println("\nOf {} pairs of households, dropping Housing increases the Euclidean "
                 "distance between shares for {} and the Aitchison distance for {}.", pairs,
                 euclid_grows, aitchison_grows);

    // 2. alr is not isometric: the same two households at different references.
    std::print("\nDistance between households 1 and 2 — Aitchison {:.4f}; "
               "alr, by reference:", dm::stats::aitchison_distance(x.row(0), x.row(1)));
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < 4; ++r) {
        const auto a = dm::stats::alr(x, r);
        std::print(" {} {:.4f}{}", names[r], euclid(a.row(0), a.row(1)),
                   r < 3 ? ";" : ".\n");
    }

    // 3. Balances from a sequential binary partition, compared between men and women.
    const Matrix partition(4, 3, {1, 1, 0, -1, 0, 1, -1, 0, -1, 1, -1, 0});
    const auto basis = dm::stats::balance_basis(partition);
    const auto z = dm::stats::ilr(x, basis);
    const char* balances[]{"Housing, Services vs Food, Other", "Housing vs Services",
                           "Food vs Other"};
    std::println("\n| Balance | Men mean | Women mean | Welch t | p |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::vector<double> men[3], women[3];
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.rows(); ++i)
        for (int b = 0; b < 3; ++b) (house[i][4] > 0 ? men : women)[b].push_back(z(i, b));
    for (int b = 0; b < 3; ++b) {
        const auto moments = [](const std::vector<double>& v) {
            double m = 0, s = 0;
            for (const double a : v) m += a / v.size();
            for (const double a : v) s += (a - m) * (a - m) / (v.size() - 1);
            return std::pair{m, s / v.size()};
        };
        const auto [m1, v1] = moments(men[b]);
        const auto [m2, v2] = moments(women[b]);
        const double t = (m1 - m2) / std::sqrt(v1 + v2);
        const double df = (v1 + v2) * (v1 + v2) / (v1 * v1 / 19 + v2 * v2 / 19);
        std::println("| {} | {:+.3f} | {:+.3f} | {:+.2f} | {:.2g} |", balances[b], m1, m2,
                     t, 2 * dm::random::student_t_cdf(-std::abs(t), df));
    }
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> by_sex[2];
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.rows(); ++i)
        by_sex[house[i][4] > 0 ? 0 : 1].push_back(x.row(i));
    for (int s = 0; s < 2; ++s) {
        Matrix g(by_sex[s].size(), 4, 0.0);
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < by_sex[s].size(); ++i)
            for (int j = 0; j < 4; ++j) g(i, j) = by_sex[s][i][j];
        const auto c = dm::stats::compositional_center(g);
        std::println("{} center (shares): Housing {:.3f}, Food {:.3f}, Other {:.3f}, "
                     "Services {:.3f}; total variance {:.3f}.", s == 0 ? "Men" : "Women",
                     c[0], c[1], c[2], c[3], dm::stats::total_variance(g));
    }

    // 4. Count zeros: the Pigs data.
    const auto pig_rows = read_rows("data/pigs.txt");
    const auto pigs = columns(pig_rows, {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5});
    int zeros = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < pigs.rows(); ++i)
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 6; ++j) zeros += pigs(i, j) == 0;
    std::println("\nPigs: {} of {} counts are zero.\n", zeros, pigs.rows() * 6);
    const auto pseudo = [](const Matrix& c, double add) {
        Matrix m = c;
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < m.rows(); ++i)
            for (std::size_t j = 0; j < m.cols(); ++j) m(i, j) += add;
        return dm::stats::closure(m);
    };
    using dm::stats::CountZeroMethod;
    const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::function<Matrix(const Matrix&)>>> methods{
        {"GBM", [](const Matrix& c) { return dm::stats::count_zero_replacement(c); }},
        {"SQ", [](const Matrix& c) { return dm::stats::count_zero_replacement(
                     c, {.method = CountZeroMethod::square_root}); }},
        {"BL", [](const Matrix& c) { return dm::stats::count_zero_replacement(
                     c, {.method = CountZeroMethod::bayes_laplace}); }},
        {"CZM", [](const Matrix& c) { return dm::stats::count_zero_replacement(
                     c, {.method = CountZeroMethod::count_zero_multiplicative}); }},
        {"Pseudo-count 0.5", [&](const Matrix& c) { return pseudo(c, 0.5); }},
        {"Pseudo-count 1", [&](const Matrix& c) { return pseudo(c, 1.0); }}};
    std::println("| Replacement | Sow 1 HALF.BED (count 0) | Sow 1 BED (count 11) | "
                 "Total variance | var log(HALF.BED/HALF.PASS) |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto& [name, f] : methods) {
        const auto r = f(pigs);
        std::println("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", name, r(0, 1), r(0, 0),
                     dm::stats::total_variance(r), dm::stats::variation_matrix(r)(1, 3));
    }

    // 5. Simulations from logistic-normal compositions with two rare parts.
    const std::size_t D = 5, rows = 60;
    const auto V = dm::stats::ilr_basis(D);
    const Matrix centre(1, D, {0.45, 0.3, 0.15, 0.07, 0.03});
    const auto mu = dm::stats::ilr(centre, V);
    const auto centre_clr = dm::stats::clr(centre);
    const double sigma = 0.5;  // ilr covariance sigma^2 I: total variance 1, every tau 0.5
    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(41);
    const auto normal = [&] {
        return dm::random::normal_quantile(rng.uniform01() * 0.999999 + 5e-7);
    };
    const auto draw = [&] {
        Matrix coords(rows, D - 1, 0.0);
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rows; ++i)
            for (std::size_t k = 0; k < D - 1; ++k)
                coords(i, k) = mu(0, k) + sigma * normal();
        return dm::stats::ilr_inverse(coords, V);
    };
    struct Score {
        double tv = 0, tau = 0, err = 0, n = 0;
        void add(const Matrix& r) {
            tv += dm::stats::total_variance(r), tau += dm::stats::variation_matrix(r)(3, 4);
            const auto c = dm::stats::clr(Matrix(1, 5, dm::stats::compositional_center(r)));
            double e = 0;
            for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 5; ++j) e += std::pow(c(0, j) - c0(0, j), 2);
            err += e, n += 1;
        }
        Matrix c0;
    };
    const auto report = [](const std::string& name, const Score& s,
                           const std::string& zeros) {
        std::println("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", name, zeros, s.tv / s.n,
                     s.tau / s.n, std::sqrt(s.err / s.n));
    };
    const int reps = 200;

    // 5a. Rounded zeros: parts 4 and 5 below limits 0.035 and 0.015 are recorded as 0.
    const std::vector<double> dl{1, 1, 1, 0.035, 0.015};
    const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, double>> fractions{
        {"Multiplicative, 0.65 DL", 0.65}, {"Multiplicative, 0.5 DL", 0.5},
        {"Multiplicative, 0.01 DL", 0.01}};
    std::vector<Score> rounded(fractions.size() + 2, Score{.c0 = centre_clr});
    double rounded_zeros = 0, dropped = 0;
    for (int rep = 0; rep < reps; ++rep) {
        const auto truth = draw();
        Matrix observed = truth;
        std::vector<std::size_t> complete;
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rows; ++i) {
            bool any = false;
            for (std::size_t j = 3; j < D; ++j)
                if (observed(i, j) < dl[j]) observed(i, j) = 0, any = true;
            for (std::size_t j = 3; j < D; ++j) rounded_zeros += observed(i, j) == 0;
            if (!any) complete.push_back(i);
        }
        rounded[0].add(truth);
        for (std::size_t f = 0; f < fractions.size(); ++f)
            rounded[f + 1].add(dm::stats::multiplicative_replacement(observed, dl,
                                                                     fractions[f].second));
        Matrix kept(complete.size(), D, 0.0);
        for (std::size_t r = 0; r < complete.size(); ++r)
            for (std::size_t j = 0; j < D; ++j) kept(r, j) = observed(complete[r], j);
        dropped += double(rows - complete.size()) / reps;
        rounded.back().add(kept);
    }
    std::println("\nRounded zeros: {:.0f} zeros per data set of {} compositions on "
                 "average; "
                 "truth total variance 1.00 and tau(part 4, part 5) 0.50.\n",
                 rounded_zeros / reps, rows);
    std::println("| Method | Rows used | Total variance | tau(part 4, part 5) | "
                 "RMSE of center (clr) |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    report("True compositions", rounded[0], std::to_string(rows));
    for (std::size_t f = 0; f < fractions.size(); ++f)
        report(fractions[f].first, rounded[f + 1], std::to_string(rows));
    report("Drop rows with zeros", rounded.back(), std::format("{:.0f}", rows - dropped));

    // 5b. Count zeros: multinomial counts of the same compositions.
    auto sim_methods = methods;
    for (const int trials : {30, 100}) {
        std::vector<Score> scores(sim_methods.size() + 1, Score{.c0 = centre_clr});
        double zero_count = 0;
        for (int rep = 0; rep < reps; ++rep) {
            const auto truth = draw();
            Matrix counts(rows, D, 0.0);
            for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rows; ++i)
                for (int t = 0; t < trials; ++t) {
                    double u = rng.uniform01(), c = 0;
                    std::size_t j = 0;
                    while (j + 1 < D && u > (c += truth(i, j))) ++j;
                    counts(i, j) += 1;
                }
            for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rows; ++i)
                for (std::size_t j = 0; j < D; ++j) zero_count += counts(i, j) == 0;
            scores[0].add(truth);
            for (std::size_t m = 0; m < sim_methods.size(); ++m)
                scores[m + 1].add(sim_methods[m].second(counts));
        }
        std::println("\nCount zeros, {} counts per composition: {:.0f} zeros per data "
                     "set.\n", trials, zero_count / reps);
        std::println("| Method | Rows used | Total variance | tau(part 4, part 5) | "
                     "RMSE of center (clr) |");
        std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
        report("True compositions", scores[0], std::to_string(rows));
        for (std::size_t m = 0; m < sim_methods.size(); ++m)
            report(sim_methods[m].first, scores[m + 1], std::to_string(rows));
    }

    // Figure: two balances, men against women, with group centers.
    std::vector<double> bx[2], by[2];
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.rows(); ++i) {
        const int s = house[i][4] > 0 ? 0 : 1;
        bx[s].push_back(z(i, 0)), by[s].push_back(z(i, 2));
    }
    const auto mean = [](const std::vector<double>& v) {
        double m = 0;
        for (const double a : v) m += a / v.size();
        return m;
    };
    auto fig = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    fig.points(bx[0], by[0], "Men", {26, 90, 147}, 4.5)
        .points(bx[1], by[1], "Women", {178, 74, 38}, 4.5)
        .points({mean(bx[0])}, {mean(by[0])}, "", {26, 90, 147}, 9)
        .points({mean(bx[1])}, {mean(by[1])}, "", {178, 74, 38}, 9)
        .abline_v(0, {180, 180, 180})
        .abline_h(0, {180, 180, 180})
        .x_limits(-0.4, 1.3)
        .y_limits(-2.8, 3.4)
        .title("Household budgets as balances")
        .x_label("Balance: Housing, Services vs Food, Other")
        .y_label("Balance: Food vs Other")
        .size(640, 430);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    fig.save_svg("build/log_ratio.svg");
}
```
