// result_end

The first table shows why raw shares are unreliable. The correlation between the Food and Other shares is −0.66 in the full budget, but −0.96 once Housing is left out, even though nothing about those two expenditures has changed. Their log-ratio variance (3.44) is identical in both, because log-ratios carry only relative information. Distances behave in the same way. Dropping Housing increases the Euclidean distance between share vectors for 485 of 780 pairs of households, but never the Aitchison distance (subcompositional dominance). The alr coordinates show the price of convenience. Depending on the reference part, the distance between the first two households ranges from 0.33 to 0.59, while the Aitchison distance is 0.33. Only clr and ilr coordinates preserve the geometry.

Balances make group comparisons interpretable, as the figure shows. The first balance contrasts Housing and Services with Food and Other. It separates the sexes almost completely (Welch t = −11.5): women allocate far more to Housing and Services relative to Food and Other. Within those groups, women spend relatively more on Housing than on Services, and men relatively more on Food than on Other. Each balance is an ordinary real-valued variable, so standard tests and models apply directly. Their conclusions are statements about ratios and do not depend on total spending.

Zeros dominate the analysis of the Pigs counts, where 50 of 174 counts are zero. The four Bayesian-multiplicative priors and two pseudo-counts replace the same zero by values from 0.0006 to 0.0097, a sixteen-fold range. The total variance of the replaced data then ranges from 5.1 to 12.7. The log-ratio variance between the two rare locations ranges from 0.45 to 5.7. Each nonzero part changes by less than 3%, but the log-ratios involving zeros depend mainly on the replacement chosen.

The simulations show which choices are defensible. For rounded zeros, the analysis can be nearly unaffected: multiplicative replacement at 0.65 of the detection limit recovers the total variance (1.03 against 0.99), the rare-part variation (0.54 against 0.50) and the center. Replacing with 0.5 of the limit inflates the dispersion moderately. Replacing with a "tiny" value, 1% of the limit, is a common practice and is disastrous: the total variance rises sixfold and the rare-part variation twelvefold. Dropping the 25% of compositions that contain zeros is also biased: it removes the compositions where the rare parts are smallest, so the dispersion shrinks (0.80) and the center error almost doubles.

Count zeros are harder, because counting noise is part of the observed variation, whatever is done about the zeros. With 30 counts per composition, every method roughly doubles the latent total variance. The more aggressive priors (SQ and BL) do so most, and a pseudo-count of 1 appears accurate only because it shrinks every ratio toward 1, at the cost of the worst center (RMSE 0.57). With 100 counts, zeros are rarer and the methods converge, while the inflation from counting noise remains. For sparse counts, a multinomial-logistic or Dirichlet-multinomial model of the counts themselves is often better than replacing zeros and treating the result as observed compositions.

>> Practical guidance

- Analyse compositions through log-ratios. Use clr for symmetric summaries and biplots, ilr balances from a sequential binary partition for models and tests, and alr only when a natural reference part exists. Do not interpret alr distances.
- Summarize with the center (closed geometric mean), the variation matrix and the total variance. These are subcompositionally coherent, while correlations of shares are not.
- Identify the origin of each zero. Use multiplicative replacement at about 0.65 of the detection limit for rounded zeros, Bayesian-multiplicative replacement (GBM by default, CZM when counts are very small) for count zeros, and amalgamation or a separate model for structural zeros (parts that are truly absent). Never use an arbitrary tiny value.
- Report the sensitivity of key results to the replacement method whenever zeros are common, as they are in the Pigs data.

